          <p>
            Dimensions for the 25 and 35 come from GE's product pages and AJ Madison, and the 50 pint figures from Total Home Supply and GE's dealer store. Notice that the 25 and 35 share a smaller chassis, while all three 50 pint versions share a taller one. If you are short on space, the 35 is the most capacity GE fits into a 19 inch tall box.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a damp basement or any space that smells musty, buy a 50. GE's Amazon listings pitch the 50 pint models for spaces up to 5,000 square feet, the 35 for 3,000 and the 25 for 1,500, but square footage matters less than dampness. A 50 run on Smart Dry will reach its target and slow down rather than waste energy, and on a GE the step from 35 to 50 costs little in size or weight. Our <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement sizing guide</a> explains the dampness classes in detail, and if you are comparing GE's 50 against other brands' at that size, see our <a href="/best-50-pint-dehumidifier/">best 50 pint dehumidifier</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 35 suits a large finished lower level that is only moderately humid, a laundry room or an open main floor. The 25 is for a single room, apartment or office, and it is the one GE model we would think twice about. GE's own page for it says it is no longer manufactured, a Best Buy listing showed it unavailable new, and at 1.70 liters per kilowatt-hour, the efficiency figure ENERGY STAR gives its ADSE25 successor, small dehumidifiers are typically less efficient per pint than 35 and 50 pint machines. For one room, also compare the options in our <a href="/best-small-dehumidifier/">small dehumidifier guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Bucket, Hose or Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every ADHE model has a front-removable bucket, and on the 50 pint models it holds 15 pints, a little under two gallons, according to Total Home Supply and GE's dealer store. When it is full, the unit stops and a full-bucket indicator lights, and GE's dealer listing describes a 10 second alarm when the bucket is full or missing. Fifteen pints is a normal size for a tower dehumidifier, but a 50 pint unit working near its rating will fill it several times a day in a wet basement, so the bucket is a backup, not a plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            All three non-pump models have a continuous drain option: you connect an ordinary garden hose to the outlet and run it downhill to a floor drain, sump or utility sink. Total Home Supply's listing for the ADHE50BWF describes this as handling up to 50 pints in 24 hours. The hose must slope downward the whole way, so the drain has to be lower than the outlet on the unit. If it is, this is the simplest and most reliable way to run any dehumidifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ADHE50PWF adds a built-in pump and includes the hose. GE's dealer store says it can drain to a location up to 16 feet away, and the Amazon listing title describes it as pumping water 15 feet vertically with a 16 foot hose. Either way, that reaches most utility sinks, laundry standpipes and basement windows. It also keeps the standard continuous drain connector, so if the pump ever fails you can switch to a gravity hose rather than being stuck with a unit that fills its bucket and stops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pumps are the part owners most often complain about on any portable dehumidifier, and GE is no exception. On Best Buy's review pages for GE's 50 pint pump models, owners describe pump lights blinking and the pump failing to empty the bucket, and one owner noted that the pump cycles on with only a little water in the bucket. If gravity drainage is possible where your unit sits, buy the ADHE50BWF and a hose instead. If it is not, our guide to <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> covers the alternatives, and <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">our pump dehumidifier roundup</a> compares GE's pump model with Midea, Frigidaire and Honeywell.
          </p>

          <p>
            One efficiency note. GE's dealer store lists the ADHE50PWF with an Integrated Energy Factor of 1.6 liters per kilowatt-hour, while Total Home Supply lists the basic and Wi-Fi 50 pint models as ENERGY STAR certified, and ENERGY STAR's current 50 pint certifications sit at 2.01. If efficiency matters to you, check the yellow EnergyGuide label on the specific listing before you buy the pump version.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smart-dry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Smart Dry, Wi-Fi and Controls
          </h2>

          <p>
            Smart Dry is GE's name for automatic fan speed. GE's dealer listing describes it as adjusting fan speed based on room humidity, so the unit runs harder when the room is far above your target and slows down as it gets close. In practice that means less noise and less energy once a basement is near the set point, without you having to change the fan speed yourself. Every ADHE model has it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the controls are conventional. Retailer listings for the 50 pint models describe three fan speeds, with airflow of 160 cubic feet per minute on low and 200 on high, a digital humidistat adjustable from 30 to 70 percent in 5 percent steps, a 24 hour on and off timer, auto restart after a power cut, and a filter reminder that lights after about 250 hours of operation. Total Home Supply lists a minimum operating temperature of 41 degrees, below which the coils can frost and performance drops, and GE's dealer store lists automatic defrost.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ADHE50WWF adds built-in Wi-Fi through GE's SmartHQ app, which lets you check room humidity, change the target and switch the unit on and off from your phone. Its Amazon listing also mentions Alexa and Google voice control. That is genuinely useful for a dehumidifier that lives in a basement or a lake house you only visit on weekends, and it adds nothing if you set the target once and leave it. The B and P versions have no Wi-Fi; Total Home Supply lists the ADHE50BWF as not Wi-Fi compatible. For a broader look at connected units, see our <a href="/best-smart-dehumidifier/">best smart dehumidifier</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is the one area where GE publishes little. We found no official decibel rating for the ADHE models; the only figure we found was a Best Buy listing that gave the ADHE50PWF a noise level of 51 decibels, without stating the fan speed or distance. GE's older ADYR22LC was listed at 43 dBA on low and 45 at maximum, but that is a different, smaller machine. Expect the 50 pint models to sound like any compressor dehumidifier on high, and noticeably quieter once Smart Dry slows the fan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Warranty, Reliability and What Owners Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read GE's warranty before you buy, because it is not what most people assume. GE's dealer store lists the ADHE50PWF with a limited one-year exchange-only parts warranty and no labor coverage, and Total Home Supply describes the ADHE50BWF and ADHE50WWF warranty as a one-year replacement for products that fail from defects in materials or workmanship. In other words, if a GE dehumidifier fails inside a year you get a replacement unit, not a repair visit, and after a year you are on your own.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because owner reports of failures around the one to two year mark come up repeatedly. On Best Buy's review pages for GE's 50 pint models, owners describe units that ran well for a while and then stopped collecting water, compressors that would not start while the fan still ran, and pump models whose pump stopped emptying the bucket. Several owners describe GE replacing or crediting failed units under warranty; others say GE does not repair dehumidifiers and offered credit toward another unit at best. These are individual accounts, not failure rates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some failures are not failures at all. A unit in a basement that has dropped into the 50s will collect much less water, because cooler air holds less moisture and the coils spend more time defrosting. A unit in a room that is already below about 45 percent humidity will run without filling the bucket quickly. Before calling GE, check the room with a separate hygrometer and make sure the filter is clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also history worth putting in context. In 2013 the Consumer Product Safety Commission announced a large recall of dehumidifiers made by Gree, which included older GE-branded units, and the recall was later expanded. Those were earlier models; the current ADHE line is not part of that recall. If you are buying a used or older GE dehumidifier, check the model number against the CPSC recall list first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our take: GE's 50 pint models are rated to dry as much air as anything in their class, but the one-year exchange warranty is short. Register the unit, keep the receipt, and if a year-two failure would be a problem for you, compare warranties carefully before you choose a brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Buy a GE, and Who Shouldn't
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a GE if you want a conventional, upright dehumidifier with straightforward controls and Smart Dry. The ADHE50BWF is a sensible default for a damp basement with a floor drain or sump nearby: run a garden hose, set it to 45 or 50 percent, and let Smart Dry manage the fan. It is also a reasonable buy on clearance, as long as you are comfortable with GE's one-year warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the ADHE50PWF if your basement has no floor drain and you can reach a sink, standpipe or window within GE's quoted pump range. Buy the ADHE50WWF if the unit will live somewhere you rarely visit and checking humidity from your phone would actually change what you do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy a GE if warranty length matters more to you than anything else; a one-year exchange is at the short end of the market. Do not buy one for an unheated space that drops below 41 degrees in winter, because that is where GE's listed operating range stops and where every compressor unit struggles. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-garage/">garage dehumidifier guide</a> and <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">crawl space guide</a> cover cold and confined spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the ADHE25BWF unless the price is very low. GE has stopped making it, its larger siblings are only a few inches taller, and for a single room there are compact options with clearer specifications. And if you empty the bucket by hand, a 15 pint bucket on a 50 pint machine means frequent trips; Midea's Cube holds far more, as we explain in our <a href="/midea-cube-dehumidifier-review/">Midea Cube review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if you need to dry a whole house rather than one level, no portable is the right tool. A ducted unit treats every room, and our comparison of <a href="/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/">whole-house versus portable dehumidifiers</a> explains when that is worth the installation cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-others" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. GE vs Midea and Frigidaire
          </h2>

          <p>
            At 50 pints, the major brands are closer than their marketing suggests. ENERGY STAR's current certifications for GE's ADSE50, Midea's 50 pint models and others cluster at about 49.6 to 49.7 pints a day and 2.01 liters per kilowatt-hour, which means they remove about the same water for about the same electricity. The real differences are the bucket, the drain options, the controls and the warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against the Midea Cube, GE's advantage is a conventional shape with three fan speeds and Smart Dry. The Cube's advantage is a far larger bucket, up to 4.25 gallons against GE's 15 pints, plus a nested shape for storage. Midea's manual rates the Cube pump to 14 feet of lift, in the same range as GE's quoted 15 to 16 feet. If you will empty by hand, the Cube is easier to live with; if you will run a hose to a drain, the difference mostly disappears. Our <a href="/midea-cube-dehumidifier-review/">Midea Cube review</a> covers that line model by model.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frigidaire's 50 pint pump model, covered in <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">our pump roundup</a>, has a larger 16.9 pint bucket and is sold almost everywhere, which makes hoses and filters easy to find. Honeywell's pump unit is rated for operation down to 41 degrees, the same as GE. None of them is a clear step up in drying; you are choosing features and support.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where GE wins is simplicity and Smart Dry. Where it loses is the short exchange-only warranty, little published noise data and a lineup in mid-transition, which makes it harder to know what you are getting from a given listing. Check the model number on the listing against the ones on this page before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Keeping a GE Dehumidifier Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the filter when the reminder lights, which GE's listings put at about every 250 hours of operation. The ADHE filter is a permanent slide-out mesh: pull it out, rinse it under the tap, let it dry and slide it back in. A clogged filter reduces airflow across the coils, which lowers water removal and makes frost more likely in a cool basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Empty and rinse the bucket regularly even if you drain by hose, because some water always collects in it. A little mild detergent and a rinse every few weeks prevents the mildew film that causes musty smells. On the pump model, check the hose for kinks and debris at the same time; a blocked hose is the simplest cause of a pump that stops moving water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Place the unit where air can move freely, away from walls and furniture, with the top discharge unobstructed. Keep doors and windows to the space closed while it runs, or it will spend its capacity drying the outdoors. If it sits on a hose, check that the hose still slopes downhill after anyone moves the unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the humidistat to 45 to 50 percent for most homes. The EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity below 60 percent and ideally between 30 and 50, and setting a GE lower than you need simply makes it run longer. Our guide to <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> goes into seasonal settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Register the unit with GE and keep the receipt. With a one-year exchange warranty, the purchase date decides whether a failure is a free replacement or a new purchase, and GE's support line for dehumidifier warranty claims is the route to that replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Amazon listing is GE's newest model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                GE's own site marks the ADHE25BWF, ADHE35BWF, ADHE50BWF and ADHE50PWF as no longer manufactured, and ENERGY STAR lists the ADSE series as their successor. The ADHE models are still worth buying, but check the model number so you know which generation you are getting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the pump model when a floor drain is close</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pump is the part owners most often report failing, and GE's dealer store lists the ADHE50PWF at an IEF of 1.6. If a garden hose can run downhill to a drain, the ADHE50BWF with a hose is simpler, more efficient and has one fewer part to break.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the warranty like a repair warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                GE lists a one-year exchange or replacement warranty, with no labor coverage on the pump model. A failure in month eleven gets you a replacement; a failure in month thirteen gets you a new purchase. Register the unit and keep the receipt from day one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in a space below 41 degrees</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Retailer listings give the ADHE models a 41 degree minimum operating temperature, and below that the coils frost and water removal drops sharply. A unit in an unheated garage or a winter crawl space will collect little water, which owners sometimes mistake for a fault.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the humidistat to its lowest setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The ADHE range goes down to 30 percent, but most homes are comfortable and mold-resistant at 45 to 50 percent. Running a GE at 30 percent keeps the compressor on almost constantly, wastes electricity and dries out wood floors and furniture with no added benefit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

